steps.text:
Risk factors from field studies:

- **slow finish**: in Boston, over 4 h against under 3:30 raised the odds about sevenfold [[c:ean-boston-time]];
- **weight gain**, and very low or very high BMI;
- **long events**: EAH is more common in ultramarathons, triathlon and open-water swimming than in marathons or cycling [[c:ean-discipline]];
- **NSAIDs**.

Slow runners have more hours to drink and, at a lower pace, sweat less.

More of the reported cases are women, but in Boston the gap disappeared once BMI and finish time were taken into account [[c:ean-sex]].

The consensus advice fits in three lines [[c:ean-prevent-rules]]:

- **drink to thirst**: the most individual strategy before, during and after exercise;
- **don't gain weight**: a gain means you've drunk more than you lost;
- **sports drinks don't protect**: they are less salty than blood, so drinking too much of them still dilutes it [[c:ean-sports-drink]].

Whether sodium supplements lower the risk is still argued [[c:ean-sodium-role]]: salt capsules raised blood sodium in a half-Ironman trial, yet sodium intake barely mattered in the 161 km race (previous lesson).

Check who paid: some planned-drinking reviews were published with sports-drink industry support. The don't-gain-weight rule is shared by both camps.
